WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
Xevious, the Avenger.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.900
Mission, seek and destroy Xevious enemy aircraft and bases.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.080
Inclusive music music

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:30.220
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.380
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:33.720
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.520
How we feeling today, guys?

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.700
What's good, what's good?

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.400
I feel spectacular, Michael.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.580
I mean, how could you not feel great after playing Xevious?

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:48.100
I feel like I don't usually just check in with you guys as a general temperature thing.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.560
No, I do appreciate it.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:58.780
I do appreciate that you actually think about us beyond just vectors for making a podcast, you know?

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:04.840
Right. Well, we've known each other for a long time outside the podcast, but we've never spoken since we started the podcast.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.160
Ever since then, it's been strictly business for the three of us.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.320
So it's good to hear that you're both well.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.940
Anybody have children or anything like that?

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.340
Nope. Okay. No children yet.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:20.720
All right. So, you know, this is like that Galaga Demons of Death game that we got earlier.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.980
And before you guys tell me why, I'm going to tell you why.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.860
It's because this is an early arcade game.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.480
They added a new subtitle to it, The Avenger, Xevious the Avenger.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:32.440
I don't know who that is.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.740
Is that like a member of Captain America's team?

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.120
It is Captain America.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:43.120
Xevious is the Avenger, yeah. So Xevious is the Avenger, or did Xevious like take down the Avengers?

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.060
And just took their name?

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.460
Well, because Xevious is the enemy aircrafts, right?

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.440
So like Xevious isn't the Avenger, right?

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.660
We are the Avengers.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.440
I thought Xevious was just like, they're all devious, you know?

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.720
Ooh, I like that.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.960
I didn't notice the rhyme there.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:10.360
Yeah. So if Xevious is the enemy aircraft, then there's no way to spin it that you're the Avenger.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:11.940
The bad guys are the Avenger.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.040
Right. That's what you would have to think.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.020
Yeah, I didn't realize.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.880
And the reason why I say that, Joe, is just because it's right on the front of the box,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.780
is that the mission is to seek and destroy Xevious enemy aircraft and bases.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.720
There's no commas, there's no periods that would like make you think that they're separate from each other.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.220
Those Xevious are the enemy aircrafts.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:35.540
Or maybe it's that like they are the Xevious Avenger, like they avenge because of the Xevious.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.060
But it says Xevious the Avenger.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.440
Okay. Not Xevious Avenger.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.200
That's true. You know what? You're right.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:47.140
And also, it could be talking about, it could just be referring to the game.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.740
Like, it's just referring to itself.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.820
Like, you gotta take out those Xevious enemies.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.040
It's like if Mario was like, you gotta take out those Super Mario Goombas.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.220
Right. Whereas, like, with Galaga, they were the demons of death.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.800
And that was very clearly communicated to the player.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.540
Where I knew right away from just starting up the first level, I was like, those are the demons of death.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.320
True. It's true.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.280
It's unclear who the Avengers is here.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:19.520
Yeah, it's true. And also, you know, the biggest reason why this is like Galaga is because there are no real updates to this game.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.720
Similar to how there were no real updates from the arcade to the NES port of Galaga.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.780
You know, this feels like I'm playing an arcade game from the early 80s.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.340
Do you guys disagree? Very much so.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:37.660
So, like, of course, like, we're finally getting back to our bread and butter, which is just shmups.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.580
And for a while there, it was like, oh, maybe there's more to the NES than shmups.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.780
Like, oh, we got a bunch of game shows now.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.400
Like, at least this is different.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.780
But, you know, they always seem to find a way to take you back.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.500
And, like, Xevious is our unhappy return, I feel.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:00.720
I was actually surprised that Wheel of Fortune didn't turn out to be a shmup.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.920
It probably has a shmup minigame hidden in there somewhere.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.080
That's what you would have been doing with the Porsche.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.520
It would have been like a Spy Hunter shmup.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.780
Exactly. You know, there was a...

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:18.660
I'll give an overview of Xevious first, but I feel like we did get an actual updated version of Xevious as a previous NES game.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.660
I want to say something like Tiger Heli came close to being, like, what an actual modern Xevious would be.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:30.700
But Xevious is an early arcade shmup that has you pitted against dozens of enemy spaceships.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:36.400
And I don't know what to call them, but the things that take place on land, they're like bases almost.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:38.080
They're like vehicles.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:39.260
Artillery. Yeah.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.960
And the game takes place entirely without interruption.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.640
There's no, like, stage one or stage two.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.500
It just loops back to the beginning after you complete a loop.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.580
Sounds very enthralling.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.200
Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.320
In fact, the music doesn't even interrupt itself from that one tiny little loop.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.400
Yeah, I think it's like a 0.8 second loop.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.800
Yeah. It really is, like, almost hypnotic.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.000
And so Tiger Heli was the game I was thinking of, but that one doesn't...

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:18.300
You can shoot on the land bases and even things in sea with your ammo.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:24.600
So what's the game I'm thinking of there that, like, lets you attack, like, in the air with land, but...

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:25.660
Oh, it was Stinger, right?

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:27.960
I think so. I think there were a few.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.980
I think there were a couple, haven't there? Yeah. There was a few that do that.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.600
Didn't that helicopter one?

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.880
Well, that's Tiger Heli.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:34.260
Oh, Tiger Heli.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:35.360
Yeah, that's right. Okay.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:38.260
Yes. So Tiger Heli, not essential.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:39.840
Stinger, essential.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:41.900
Yeah. Got it.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:49.460
Yeah, I don't really know what makes Xevious special as an NES game.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.320
Is this just... Do you think this is a thing that, like, this was put on the system because it was a very...

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.700
And truly, it was a very popular arcade game back in the early 80s.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:07.380
And maybe, like, someone finally now wants it for their home because arcade machines are, like, you know, $2,000 or...

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:13.020
I'm making numbers, but, like, you know, they're very expensive and it's, like, finally we can give it to them on the NES, right?

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.100
Like, is that the logic?

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:24.880
That's really all... That's all I can think of as a reason because there are enough of these experiences on the system already, like, as we've notified.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:30.200
But I guess this specific game hasn't been on here yet.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.300
So it's just, yeah, any classic, they have to fill out their catalog.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:43.980
But that's just me as somebody that has never played the arcade game Xevious and not really knowing the full experience.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:45.260
No, I'm telling you, it's this.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:46.480
It's this, Sean. It's just this.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:47.680
I've played it and it is this.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.600
Even graphically, it looks very much like this.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:54.640
Wow. Well, that's all I can think of then.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.460
I think that that is exactly what happened.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:02.080
Like, it's just for people who loved it in the arcade, like you said, they want to own it.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:16.140
But I also think another factor that might factor in is just that in 1988, gamers, like, general gamers didn't have, like, the collective knowledge that we have now of every other game that's out.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.340
And, like, you buy a game, you're like, oh, this is a cool game where you fly a ship and you shoot.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.720
You're not really, like, aware of, you're not really thinking, like, oh, man, there's been so many of these.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:28.120
I imagine you wouldn't be so aware because you just didn't have access to every game.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.660
And you weren't watching videos of the games you didn't own.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.120
And you weren't, like, you know, you had the games that you owned and the games that your friends owned.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:43.420
So maybe it was, like, it was a lot more forgivable for there to be so many similar games because you just had less access to them.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.060
I'd say I agree with that.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:57.720
And, you know, we're in a particular time period for video gaming right now with remakes, remasters, and ports, like, the fine line between those three things and what they are.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.360
And I think it would just be funny to think about Xevious here.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:09.140
And it's, like, from a purist perspective, is it better that they left this game mostly untouched for fans of Xevious as an NES thing?

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:18.660
Like, should they have added a separate mode or, like, should they have modeled it after the other shmups that are on this system and included, like, stages and power-ups?

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:21.820
Or was this just, like, this is just for the purists?

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.760
I think there's two ways of looking at that.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:26.800
You know, I think you just kind of spelled them out there, too.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:32.880
I think if you want this because you loved it in the arcade and you want to go back and play your favorite game, I don't think you want...

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.260
You think you want to, like, just play that again?

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:39.400
Because, you know, how much access to, like, the arcade version do you have?

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.680
But then there's also...

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.860
I think there's a lot of...

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:48.540
I don't know. There could be a lot of desire for, like, an updated version of it.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:49.520
I loved that game back then.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.660
How does it age now compared to games that I've played since?

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.960
I think it's just a preference.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.620
When it comes to home ports of arcade games,

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:09.740
it's sort of been a crapshoot when it comes to quality and how well it actually remakes the experience.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.300
Or, like, how well it ports the experience.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:18.860
And if this is as much of a one-to-one port as you're saying, Mike,

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.000
I would say that, yeah, if I was a fan of this game in the arcades,

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:30.400
I would be more than happy to play it as is at home without spending dollars of quarters just to keep going.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.520
So I guess that's one way to look at it.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:37.420
Yeah, and even from, like, a purist's point of view,

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.720
you know, going off of what Sean was saying there,

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:43.540
even Nintendo can't seem to get their arcade ports exactly right,

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:48.760
with Donkey Kong being limited to missing an entire stage of that arcade game.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:52.820
So there's definitely, you know, a thinking here,

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.400
where I think Joe was touching upon,

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:59.400
where people don't know everything that's happened in video games like we do now, you know?

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.180
And we're kind of obsessive about it.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:03.760
I'm not sure, like, the general gamer knows this stuff either.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.240
But, you know, for some people, they would buy Xevious,

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.280
not know that there was an arcade game in the early, like, you know, 10 years earlier,

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.220
and they would enjoy this game.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:14.720
They wouldn't really find many problems with it

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.560
because they didn't own all the other shmups that were on the NES.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:23.540
Yeah. I think that because we have the entire catalog in front of us,

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:27.400
we can, we make a lot of different judgments

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:32.000
that players may not have made back when they were being released.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.660
So having played through the game,

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.680
would you say that if you had spent money on this and knowing what you know,

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:40.780
you would feel burned, no?

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:46.620
I guess, like, if I didn't have a special relationship with Xevious Arcade,

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:52.320
I wouldn't be all that pleased with what I got.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:57.440
Because this is just, it seems like another bare-bones shmup,

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:03.000
like another, like, template shmup that didn't get all of the bells and whistles

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.620
that the new ones, or the newer ideas usually get.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.240
I don't know how you guys felt.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:10.000
Yeah, I agree.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.760
I think that the only way that you're pleased, really pleased with this purchase

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:20.400
is if you set out, like, for nostalgic reasons,

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.160
you wanted to play this old game, you know,

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.860
completely, fully will keep my interest for a long time.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.460
And so let's get into that actual gameplay.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:38.880
You know, there's really, the thing that sets it apart from some other shmups

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:42.220
is that thing we talked about where there's shooting and there's bombing.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:48.080
You bomb the land enemies and you shoot the aerial enemies.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:52.520
There's a weird, like, lock-on system for that

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:55.860
where you can see where your bombs are going to land,

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:57.660
but you can't move or control that.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:02.020
It's just like, if a land enemy happens to be in that crosshair,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.560
that's your perfect time to bomb them?

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.160
It's basically a reticle.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.760
And it's, it, I wouldn't imagine that you could adjust it.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:15.640
Like, if you treat it as, like, the reticle in a first-person shooter nowadays,

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:18.160
like, you can't move your crosshair.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.660
You just have to put your enemy in the crosshair.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:22.980
So I think that that's totally fine.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:28.000
And I guess it is unique, at least in that it gives you that information,

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.780
unlike, I think, maybe Tiger Heli and some of the other shmups.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.140
Just, like, you just had to eyeball it.

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.640
Right. Were those ones, were you actually dropping bombs

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.020
and they would hit at a certain point?

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:43.700
Or would, if an enemy on the ground crossed over the trajectory of your bomb,

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:49.240
That's more for games like Gradius and Life Force.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:51.160
Yeah, the side ones. With the missiles, yeah.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:54.860
Right, right. But there were some where you had to bomb directly.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:59.760
Right. Yeah, it would, like, go over their head if it wasn't that distance away.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:03.920
Gotcha. Yeah, the crosshair didn't bother me too much.

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:06.620
Maybe it's just because we're not used to it.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:07.700
It was a little weird.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:10.880
It just, maybe, and also because it's, like, on an NES game,

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:14.820
everything's just so much clunkier than we're used to,

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.180
that, like, having a crosshair that is, like, the exact,

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.020
almost the same size of your ship moving at this,

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.160
it felt like it, like, clutters the screen a little bit.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:25.560
But that's me kind of looking for, like, things wrong with it.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:28.400
Otherwise, like, it didn't, I don't think it was too bad.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:34.660
Well, sort of, on that note, just talking about the visual look of this game,

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:41.020
it is sort of, it's sort of unique in that it's got this, like,

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:44.940
pseudo vector thing going on where it is super clean.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:50.920
It's the cleanest looking game that I've seen on this system to a fault,

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:56.940
where it's basically just straight lines and solid colors.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:01.720
And I think that any busyness that you would see with, like,

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:06.840
this extra reticle is completely countered by the fact that most of this game

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:08.240
has zero texture.

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.540
Yeah, I'm glad you put it that way, too,

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.740
because I was trying to find that, like, trying to really, like,

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.840
articulate my, what was grabbing me about, like,

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:19.140
what felt off about this.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:21.800
And it was kind of like what you said, was that the,

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:24.440
it's so, it's so...

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.660
It's, like, clean. Yeah.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:29.660
Yeah, it's so clean that, like, it's all solid colors that it,

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:32.840
at first it looks like, wow, this is, like, really bright and colorful.

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:36.120
And, like, there's no, like, weird glitchy textures,

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:39.060
which, you know, things that might look kind of crappy on the NES.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.420
But then because there's none of that texture stuff,

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:42.800
it almost looks like,

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.300
it looks like something I would have made in, like,

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.480
Microsoft Paint is what I keep thinking of.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:49.660
Like, it looks really, really, like,

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:53.080
maybe it's because we're just conditioned to remember that kind of stuff

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:54.520
from, like, our old computers,

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:59.400
but it just looks very, like, like a kid drew it, you know?

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:04.080
Yeah, even the enemy, the enemy art,

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.680
it's mostly just circles with bullet,

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:09.760
like, with things that bullets come out of in the center.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.900
Right. Yeah, and you don't even see, like, an actual spot

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:14.520
for where those bullets might come out.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:17.580
They just come from the center of every single enemy.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.380
There's no ship design per se.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:25.500
And, yeah, really the only, like, nice sprite work is your ship,

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:27.900
which makes sense because it's on there the whole time.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:32.120
But I have this weird feeling of, like, looking at the, you know,

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:33.080
like, looking at the ground,

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:36.680
and it just keeps giving me this idea of, like, baseball fields.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.420
Yeah. I can kind of see that.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:43.380
And there's also this whole thing of, like, I don't know,

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:46.940
you guys didn't own an Intellivision or an Atari, right?

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:48.220
You guys didn't have those? You don't know me.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:49.220
No. Okay.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:53.840
Well, I had a pretty extensive Intellivision collection for some reason.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:57.280
Wow. This game kind of looks like an Intellivision game,

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.220
and that's not a compliment.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:01.980
No, I mean, I'm not saying that it's nice.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:05.600
I'm just saying that it looks, yeah, too clean.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.080
But, yeah, I know what you're saying.

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:12.520
And, Joe, you had mentioned a long time ago on our Star Force episode,

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.040
I don't know if you remember that game or not,

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:18.420
but Star Force was the shmup with no power-ups.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:22.380
And you said, I could see this as more of, like, for a purist, you know,

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:25.100
or somebody who just wants to learn how to be good at shmups.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.860
Does that change at all for you for Xevious?

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.580
I think for Xevious it does change because this isn't, like, a particularly,

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:36.340
in my opinion, as someone who doesn't like shmups in general,

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.220
this isn't a particularly fun one.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:43.380
I thought I could see Star Force being more, I don't know,

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:44.680
it feels like more of a challenge.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.600
It still feels like a little more of a traditional shmup.

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:52.880
I mean, this feels, you're just moving very slowly.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.340
You've got that repetitive music.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:57.180
A lot of the, maybe it's because I'm not good at it,

00:16:57.260 --> 00:17:00.920
but a lot of the firepower coming at you, to me,

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:04.580
felt so much harder to dodge between than in other games we've had.

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:06.280
Oh, yeah. Like, it's harder to see.

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:07.920
It's really dense.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:11.580
It just, I don't know, I wasn't getting it from this.

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:13.540
Yeah, it's still a very difficult game,

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:19.520
so I don't know if, like, that whole Star Force is more of a challenge statement

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:21.040
is all that true.

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:24.400
But at least, I can't remember Star Force,

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:28.080
but I can only imagine it had a little bit more personality than this one.

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:32.400
I wouldn't say that I'm trying to say that Star Force was more challenging.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:35.620
I think it's just more that it was challenging in the ways that it was, like,

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:37.000
designed to be challenging,

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:39.500
and not, like, this one's challenging because it's just, like,

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.140
crazy stuff's coming at you too much, or, like,

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:46.580
it felt like there was a little more intent in what was making Star Force challenging,

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:49.620
basing this off of my limited memory of what that was.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:53.280
But that's how I remember feeling about it by the time.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.800
Yeah, did you guys notice that, you know, the way that enemies attack you,

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:02.100
it's very, uh, it's kind of poorly designed, I'm going to throw it out there,

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:06.200
because it's all OEs in relation to where you currently are.

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.440
So if you are on, like, the far right corner,

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:13.340
and you drag yourself along from right to left,

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.860
all of the bullets that come at you from any particular enemy

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:19.040
will follow that same curve with you,

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:20.820
and yeah, they might eventually catch up with you or something,

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:23.800
but I just think it's weird that their only point of target

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.960
is just wherever you currently are.

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:28.660
There's no, like, enemy that shoots this way,

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:30.840
that, like, then comes around the other way, you know?

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:32.380
Like, we've seen patterns and shmups,

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.960
and this one just seems to be more guaranteed to be, like,

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.740
wherever you are, that's where we're coming for.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:40.900
Yeah, I noticed that too, because what stuck out to me

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:44.040
was that you have to constantly be moving back and forth,

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:46.520
because if you stay in, like, one point,

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:49.140
all of the firepower will just be concentrated on you,

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:52.060
and if you wait too long, there's, like, almost no way to get past it,

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:53.500
because it's all coming at you.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:56.060
I mean... So I felt like you had to just constantly be moving around

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:58.140
and just, like, in, like...

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:00.800
You could even move in circles and avoid this stuff, you know?

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:06.420
I'm not gonna say that, like, this is a great game

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:07.520
in terms of game design,

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:12.380
but I will at least say that that is a design decision.

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:13.740
Like, for it to be...

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:15.380
To make sure that you have to keep moving,

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:18.500
I'm not gonna say that that's an intrinsically bad thing

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:19.920
about the game.

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:24.240
It may not be as enjoyable than learning patterns

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:25.380
and then just sort of, like,

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:27.220
staying out of that pattern's way,

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:31.880
but I think that it's not a fault of the game

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:33.920
that it's, like, sort of tracking you.

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:36.260
I think that that is its own kind of game.

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:38.520
Sure. Yeah, I see your point there.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:40.360
Yeah, I think that's fair. I was just trying...

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:42.780
I was just saying it was an observation more than anything.

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:44.640
I definitely think that's a valid point of yours

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.140
to say that that was a design decision.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:47.260
You have to...

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:49.080
It's not a random chance.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:50.960
You have to program them to do so.

00:19:51.520 --> 00:20:00.100
Yeah. Did anybody notice that...

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:02.020
Not to jump ahead to, I guess, what we would...

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:03.700
I don't know if you'd even call them boss battles,

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:06.020
but those giant things in the ground,

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:07.920
I guess they're boss battles. You're talking about Andor Genesis?

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:10.620
Excuse me? I suppose.

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:14.780
That's what they're called in the manual.

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.920
It's the big boss, the big base, if you will.

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:22.420
Andor Genesis. And that's the only time your screen stops scrolling

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:25.280
is when you have to deal with Andor Genesis,

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:26.660
which I think is just such a cool name,

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:27.800
so I'm going to keep saying it.

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:29.120
Joe, what were your thoughts on it?

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:31.480
Well, I was going to say that that is where,

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:34.080
I think the culmination of what we're talking about here

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:35.440
is where I really noticed it.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:38.640
I thought that was one of the most difficult shmup bosses

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:41.400
we've ever faced, including the double volcano from...

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:42.460
Double volcano.

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:46.120
Yeah. What about upside down double volcano?

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.340
Yeah. Let's not go too far.

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:50.440
But it was...

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:51.520
But again, I mean,

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.520
I guess to Sean's point,

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:57.900
it is not necessarily bad design.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:00.340
It was just so difficult because

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:02.800
it was just like,

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:07.300
it felt like constant walls of these tiny little balls of bullets

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:11.960
or whatever they are. And there was like almost no room to get through there.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:13.760
And it just felt like there was nowhere to go.

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:15.660
Like they were just, everything was honing in on you.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:18.400
And it like didn't give you anything to work with.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:21.380
Again, maybe that's just because I'm not great at this game,

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:23.480
but I feel like that was like difficult

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:25.380
in a way that was like...

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:26.280
No, it definitely was.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:27.840
I didn't feel like I could come up with like a good strategy.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:30.300
I just had to kind of really just like hope

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:32.880
and like dodge as best I can.

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:36.560
But... Yeah, I agree that it was very difficult.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:40.100
And I think part of the reason why it's so difficult

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:44.000
is because there's no variety

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:49.280
in terms of like the shit that's being shot at you.

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:54.260
Like everything, whether it's like these floating bowling balls

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:57.040
that dip into and out of space

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:58.320
or the giant bosses,

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:01.780
they're all shooting these same bullets at you

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:04.380
with no variety as to like how big they are,

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:06.480
how they move, how fast they are.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.940
And when you just pile on

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:15.120
and it's just like dozens of bullets in the air

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:16.460
all going at you,

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:18.860
I think that that's sort of like lazy design

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:21.280
and also just it overwhelms the player.

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:22.400
I mean, yeah, there are a lot of people

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:23.540
that are very good at this game,

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:25.160
but I...

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:28.900
It just seems broken in that regard

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:31.360
where it's like these bosses are...

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:32.600
It's just too much. I don't know.

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:35.280
Yeah, and Andor Genesis has me thinking about

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:38.360
like the spray of these bullets too

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:39.780
because we're used to,

00:22:39.980 --> 00:22:41.260
in most of our shmup games,

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.540
but especially in the good ones,

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:46.020
we're used to a spray on these bullets

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:48.840
that allows you to kind of weave through them.

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:50.340
And that's not the case here

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:51.860
because as Sean mentioned,

00:22:52.180 --> 00:22:54.160
the bullets are so small,

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:56.100
the projectiles, whatever you want to call them,

00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:59.140
they're so small. And there is chance for overlapping and stuff like that

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:01.560
that in some ways, they kind of create like a wall

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:03.640
that blocks you off from moving

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.500
in a particular direction.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.540
And you have to...

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:10.780
There's no dodging. It's more like just, you know,

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:13.580
like moving against the grain of the shooting.

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:16.700
Mm-hmm. Yeah, and just making sure that

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:20.540
you get into that one tiny like door.

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:22.500
Right. And I don't know.

00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:24.700
Yeah. Yeah, and I also noticed that

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:26.200
there were like points

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.020
that were really difficult,

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:30.040
like aside from the boss battles,

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:32.880
really difficult because there's dense firepower everywhere.

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:34.820
And then there'd be like long periods of time

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:35.960
that felt like a rest,

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:37.780
which isn't necessarily bad,

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:40.500
but just felt like the difficulty was very lopsided

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:41.940
where like sometimes it would be like,

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.060
oh my God, I'll never get past this part.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:45.400
And then like for the next,

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:46.440
you know, three minutes,

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:48.880
it's like really easy. And then it's incredibly hard again.

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:51.240
It just felt like, and maybe that is partially

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:52.420
because it has to do with

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:53.400
where you're flying

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:54.860
and when things are shooting at you,

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:57.060
but it just didn't feel like

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:58.600
it was getting progressively more difficult.

00:23:58.600 --> 00:23:59.560
It just felt like it was like,

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:01.500
sometimes it's really hard and sometimes it's not so hard.

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:03.320
I think it's just a,

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.700
like a not so perfect implementation

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.960
of like the concept quiet time,

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:11.360
which exists more in like more modern games

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:12.460
and less in arcades

00:24:12.460 --> 00:24:18.960
because you're paying for like this high adrenaline experience.

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:22.060
But I don't think it's a,

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:24.000
it's a necessarily bad thing

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.400
that there are these weird lulls,

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:29.960
but the timing and spacing of those lulls

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:31.520
is, yeah, erratic at that.

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:34.140
Did you guys happen to notice

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:36.900
the way that some of the spaceships

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:38.140
are designed?

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:40.620
You know, they kind of resemble

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:42.820
some other science fiction works,

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:44.540
like the X-Wings

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:45.600
from Star Wars.

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:47.960
Apparently, there's some

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:49.200
Battlestar Galactica

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:51.160
inspired ships as well.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:52.980
There's the UFOs,

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:53.920
which are just, you know,

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:55.680
the common saucer things.

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:57.700
They, you know,

00:24:57.760 --> 00:24:58.900
they acknowledge this

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:01.700
in their, in their development process.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:02.980
They said that they pretty much

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:04.160
use those X-Wings

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:05.400
as models and stuff.

00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:07.000
I don't mind that at all,

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:08.040
but I do think it's kind of weird

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:09.600
to have like these homages

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:11.540
to so many different sci-fi works

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:13.320
all in one piece.

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:15.180
Yeah, I,

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:17.080
I guess I didn't really notice

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:18.620
just because they're so small

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:20.980
and moving so fast

00:25:20.980 --> 00:25:23.160
that you can't, aside from these weird,

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.700
like, they look like CDs

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:26.980
just flying in the air

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:28.020
that are,

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:29.360
I think it's your first enemy.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:31.620
I couldn't really

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:32.800
tell the difference

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:35.600
between them. Aside,

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:37.820
yeah, aside from the, the other weird bowling balls

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:39.840
that would just like phase in and out of existence.

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:41.940
Right. The,

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:43.740
the X-Wings you, I would notice at least

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:44.860
because, um,

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:45.980
that's just something

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:46.980
very familiar to me,

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:48.020
but also they are

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:48.980
the most aggressive.

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:50.680
They, they come in from the top

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:52.200
and they, they kind of

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:53.500
just come at like

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:55.420
top speed directly at you.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:56.840
They're the only enemy like that.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:58.300
Most, most enemies come in

00:25:58.300 --> 00:25:59.220
either from the sides

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:00.420
or they stay on screen

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:02.660
for a while. The X-Wings just kind of

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:04.180
fly by and then that's it.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:06.020
Huh. What about the,

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:07.100
uh, what about the tumbling

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:08.640
rectangular gray boards?

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:10.480
Yeah. What sci-fi is that?

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:11.680
I think that's,

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:13.620
uh, like what I want to say,

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:14.760
I don't know if it is,

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:15.880
but it,

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:16.880
they look very much

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:17.960
like monoliths.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:19.100
That's what I was going to say

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:19.900
from 2001.

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:21.220
Space Odyssey, yeah. Oh,

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:22.240
gotcha. I didn't expect that

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:23.480
there would be an answer to that, but that's a good,

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:24.880
that's a good point. Yeah,

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:26.040
no, that's what I was thinking too

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:29.000
and that's funny. you know,

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:30.660
but there's, but there's limited amounts

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:32.180
of enemies too. It's not like,

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:33.580
you know, if they wanted to embrace

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:34.980
all these different science fiction works,

